we begin the day with russia's wish list  for its future dealings with the west  today moscow published a set of security  proposals that aim to limit nato's  expansion into former soviet countries  the documents were handed to the united  states earlier this week president putin  is demanding legally binding guarantees  that nato would give up any military  activity in eastern europe and ukraine  the line pursued by the united states  and nato over recent years  to aggressively escalate the security  situation  is absolutely unacceptable and extremely  dangerous  washington and its nato allies should  immediately stop hostile actions against  our country  including unscheduled war games  dangerous encounters  and maneuvers of military ships and  planes  and stop for military use of ukrainian  territory  nato's leadership and the white house  have indicated that they are open to  talks with russia but they insist that  every country has the right to determine  its own future without outside  interference and the alliance has  rejected what it says amounts to a veto  on its expansion  we have received  russia's recent proposals  we are clear that any dialogue with  russia would also need to address nato's  concerns  about  russia's actions  be based on the core principles  and documents of european security and  take place in consultation with nato's  european partners such as ukraine  well tensions between russia and the  west have soared over a recent build-up  of russian troops near ukraine  triggering fears of an invasion moscow  denies any plan to attack its neighbour  despite mounting evidence to the country  this is some of the latest video from  russia's board of regents shot by a  french television crew about eight  kilometers from ukraine according to the  reporter this normally functions as an  occasional training area for the russian  army but for weeks now they've been  massing troops and equipment essentially  turning it into a military base  so let's take a closer look at these  russian demands and nato's responses  with dw correspondent terry schultz  welcome terry let's start with more  about russia's about what russia has  been asking for  well phil you know we've been hearing  these complaints from moscow for years  about how nato should not be expanding  to countries that were in the former  soviet sphere of influence so they  didn't really come as a surprise most of  them at the same time  when you see things written down when  they are delivered in the form of  proposed treaties one between the u.s  and russia and one between nato and  russia it takes on a different a  different flavor and i think it was  pretty pretty bold of president putin to  write out these these supposed treaties  and hand them over now in the midst of  such tense times  and was there an or else in this stated  or implied  i think there are tens of thousands of  or else's masked along the border with  ukraine russia again  as i mentioned it it prepared these  treaties for the first time written down  spelled out in such detail while it has  possibly more than a hundred thousand  soldiers  massing along multiple points on the  border with ukraine and i heard even as  recently as today from a nato diplomat  that despite the phone call between  presidents biden and putin despite all  the talk about  wanting to de-escalate that russia is  instead escalating that it is moving  more and more material troops and  long-term  long-term facilities into this region  keeping nato and the u.s very nervous  right so on the face of it though  amongst these demands like demanding a  veto on nato members and telling nato  where it can and can't deploy its forces  these sound like demands that moscow  expects to fail or to be rejected so can  we speculate about what president putin  really wants  well some of them are not ones that that  russia expects to fail some of them of  course are i mean one of the most um i  think outrageous ones is that it says  that nato cannot uh deploy in any sort  of resources in countries that are nato  members like the baltic states any any  country that joined after 1997 which  they did so i mean some of those  obviously are non-starters others are  actually things that nato would like to  see that the u.s would like to see um  limitations on on what kind of missiles  can be based where and used and  especially as you heard secretary  general stoltenberg say  confidence-building measures now this is  something that nato has been asking from  russia for years about more transparency  about what kind of exercises that it's  launching in countries uh on near the  border of of nato allies  in belarus  for example and russia has been simply  unresponsive to these requests from nato  so sure some of them if russia were to  undertake them with any genuine interest  would be something nato would be  interested in discussing but that  remains to be seen as i say russia has  refused to for example allow observers  to assess exercises as it is required to  do under international agreements so  there actually could be some things to  talk about that would be good for both  sides  and how have nato representatives been  responding to these demands  you heard the secretary general say um  as i just mentioned that there are  things that nato would like to talk  about at the same time there are things  there are issues such as uh giving  moscow a veto over which countries can  join nato that will never ever be  something  that can even be put on the table in any  way shape or form um russia will not  have the right to say that ukraine can't  become a nato member or certainly that  any sort of deployments must be pulled  out of of nato allies but  written into these proposed treaties are  also some measures that um could be  discussed but i just reread them before  we were coming on the air and some of  them even already exist for example that  hotlines would be set up between nato  and russia in case um to to avoid any  sort of mistakes or incidents that  already exists so some of these are kind  of puzzling to to me  meanwhile russia of course is massing  troops its own troops on its own  territory  menacingly close to ukraine which isn't  a nato member so has nato said  what these massive consequences will be  or this high price that russia will pay  if russia does in fact invade ukraine  trust me phil we ask constantly what  exactly they would do and of course  they will not divulge what they are  preparing for in case  russia were to invade ukraine i mean it  is said constantly that because ukraine  is not a nato member there's no article  5 guarantee for that country it cannot  call the nato allies to defend it in  case of further aggression in case of an  invasion by russia and ukraine is very  painfully aware of this at the same time  if russia were to step across the border  to do more than it's already done in  taking crimea ukraine's crimea region  the west could not fail to respond that  would simply be too great of of of an  aggression against  against a partner country of nato allies  of the united states so i think that  one important point is that for example  last night the european union and nato  put out statements talking about  repercussions for moscow the european  union could take could take very extreme  financial measures that could cut off  for example russian banks that would be  the most the the most instant response  that could be done by the international  community and perhaps the the one that  russia would feel uh the most keenly  nato is unlikely to mount a military  response to a russian invasion of  ukraine nonetheless most of the allies  are also members of the european union  plus the united states and they have  ways of making moscow feel pain if it  were to take this step and truly go back  into ukraine all right we shall watch  and wait for now terry thank you so much  terry schultz in brussels  you
 

